1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:02,400
In this video, I'm
going to talk to you

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,155
about Linux device update.

3
00:00:04,155 --> 00:00:05,790
This is the first
of a multi-part

4
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:06,870
series where we talk about

5
00:00:06,870 --> 00:00:10,455
how to update embedded
Linux devices.

6
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:13,140
In this first one, we're
just going to talk about

7
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:14,700
some common design goals

8
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,860
for embedded Linux
design update,

9
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:18,120
and then in the
next videos we're

10
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:19,350
going to talk more
about how you can

11
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:22,750
actually pull this off
in an implementation.

12
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:25,710
Starting first
defining the problem

13
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:27,735
and the solution set here,

14
00:00:27,735 --> 00:00:29,580
here's an overview of

15
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:33,135
a common device update scenario

16
00:00:33,135 --> 00:00:34,500
that you'll have if you're

17
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,765
developing an embedded
Linux based device.

18
00:00:36,765 --> 00:00:38,640
You've probably got
some Cloud server

19
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,485
that's a part of
the implementation,

20
00:00:40,485 --> 00:00:42,260
you have some
development environment

21
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:44,030
where you're working
on the code,

22
00:00:44,030 --> 00:00:45,395
writing the code that's going to

23
00:00:45,395 --> 00:00:46,940
ultimately run on the device,

24
00:00:46,940 --> 00:00:48,530
and you got Device Update File,

25
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:51,260
which is a series of ones
and zeros that need to

26
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:54,520
get programmed on the
device in some method.

27
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,520
This could contain some
different subset of components,

28
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,050
including bootloaders,

29
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:02,555
root file system
components, et cetera.

30
00:01:02,555 --> 00:01:04,550
But the ultimate goal is to get

31
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:07,480
that onto the device that
you're implementing.

32
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,470
You obviously have some
kind of device that's doing

33
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:12,620
some function and likely
internet connected.

34
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:15,290
Then you may have something
like a wireless router in

35
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:16,520
the mix depending on how

36
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:17,860
you're actually
connected to the device,

37
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:18,890
but something that gets you

38
00:01:18,890 --> 00:01:20,600
onto a network that

39
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,855
will give you access
to the World Wide Web.

40
00:01:22,855 --> 00:01:25,520
The ultimate goal here is to get

41
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,714
this Device Update
File onto the device,

42
00:01:28,714 --> 00:01:31,050
probably through the Cloud.

43
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:34,475
When we talk about
updating something,

44
00:01:34,475 --> 00:01:36,080
especially over a
wireless connection,

45
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:39,815
you'll often see the acronym
OTA as Over The Air updates.

46
00:01:39,815 --> 00:01:42,590
So we're just really
referring to this more

47
00:01:42,590 --> 00:01:44,180
generically as updating software

48
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:46,210
on an embedded device
after shipment.

49
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:47,990
It's commonly referred to as

50
00:01:47,990 --> 00:01:49,700
OTA for wireless applications,

51
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,740
you'll actually see
it referred to as OTA

52
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:53,930
for Wired applications as well.

53
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:57,295
It's just a normal way to
talk about device updates.

54
00:01:57,295 --> 00:01:59,090
There are a couple
of different things

55
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:00,260
that you want to
think about when

56
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:03,545
you're designing the
device update scheme.

57
00:02:03,545 --> 00:02:05,120
One of them is security,

58
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:06,500
and we'll talk about
how you can use

59
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,280
asymmetric cryptography
to accomplish this.

60
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:10,860
Second is reliability.

61
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:13,280
So you want to be able to
handle different scenarios that

62
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:16,180
could come up during
the update process.

63
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:17,970
Then the third is ease of use.

64
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:19,490
So you don't want
to add burdens to

65
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:21,770
your users or to your
development team,

66
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:23,765
you want to make
this as seamless and

67
00:02:23,765 --> 00:02:27,330
as much of a part of the
normal workflow as possible.

68
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:31,865
When you're developing
your embedded application,

69
00:02:31,865 --> 00:02:35,840
you'll find some trade-offs
like this in terms

70
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,130
of trading off

71
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:40,250
implementation complexity
with your solution,

72
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:43,475
with how easy it is
to use that solution.

73
00:02:43,475 --> 00:02:47,570
There's a few different
areas where you can trade

74
00:02:47,570 --> 00:02:52,130
off these differences
between update schemes.

75
00:02:52,130 --> 00:02:54,510
Thinking in terms of

76
00:02:54,510 --> 00:02:56,870
how you could minimize

77
00:02:56,870 --> 00:02:59,105
the amount of work that you
need to do with your team,

78
00:02:59,105 --> 00:03:01,460
there are solutions out there

79
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:04,265
that implement Device
Update for you.

80
00:03:04,265 --> 00:03:07,925
One example is
Android or Particle.

81
00:03:07,925 --> 00:03:10,370
Where if you have the
hardware support and you have

82
00:03:10,370 --> 00:03:13,550
the capability to
use a platform like

83
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:16,760
this and the financial means

84
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:19,610
to do so with respect to
the product development,

85
00:03:19,610 --> 00:03:20,930
then you can have kind of a more

86
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:22,790
turn-key solution where
there's some level

87
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:24,290
of update built directly into

88
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:26,705
whatever you're
better platform is.

89
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:29,780
Then getting down into

90
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:30,950
some of the details
about how you

91
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:32,410
connect to the product,

92
00:03:32,410 --> 00:03:36,200
there are connectivity methods

93
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,070
that make it easier
to do Device Update.

94
00:03:38,070 --> 00:03:39,530
In the sense that there's

95
00:03:39,530 --> 00:03:42,410
less work for your users to
do and there's less work for

96
00:03:42,410 --> 00:03:43,790
your developers to do in

97
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:45,080
terms of actually
being able to pull

98
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:48,735
off embedded device updates.

99
00:03:48,735 --> 00:03:51,785
Thinking of standards
like Wi-Fi and Bluetooth,

100
00:03:51,785 --> 00:03:53,030
and the ability to

101
00:03:53,030 --> 00:03:55,015
connect to a device
through a mobile phone,

102
00:03:55,015 --> 00:03:57,080
for instance, if you can use

103
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:58,970
those as a part of
your updates scheme,

104
00:03:58,970 --> 00:04:01,100
then you can probably
make it easier

105
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,390
to roll out for users,

106
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:06,200
and you can probably make
it a little bit easier

107
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:10,535
for developers to
interact with the device.

108
00:04:10,535 --> 00:04:12,890
There's maybe some
implementation trade-off

109
00:04:12,890 --> 00:04:14,300
where there's little
more work to do with

110
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:18,890
the solution to make it work
over a protocol like these.

111
00:04:18,890 --> 00:04:22,250
Then on the low end
of the spectrum,

112
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:25,580
in terms of overall cost
and development effort,

113
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:29,675
you might have things like
serial cables or USB cables.

114
00:04:29,675 --> 00:04:31,775
Lot of times when you're
using a development kit,

115
00:04:31,775 --> 00:04:33,430
this is where everything starts.

116
00:04:33,430 --> 00:04:35,230
As you plug a USB device in

117
00:04:35,230 --> 00:04:37,365
and you plug into the product,

118
00:04:37,365 --> 00:04:40,650
maybe you push some buttons
to get in a reset mode,

119
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:42,320
and then you can use
some built-in tools

120
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,555
provided by the manufacturer.

121
00:04:44,555 --> 00:04:48,425
That often works okay
for development process,

122
00:04:48,425 --> 00:04:49,790
but if you want to use that for

123
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:51,380
updating devices in the field,

124
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:53,870
there are often some
pretty serious limitations

125
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:54,890
with those.

126
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:56,720
You're often going to need to do

127
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:58,250
a little bit development work to

128
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:00,440
get at least somewhere
in this level,

129
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:03,500
and maybe up here
depending on what

130
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:07,470
you're using for
your implementation.

131
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:10,360
A couple of things to talk

132
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:12,160
about in terms of the goals and

133
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:16,465
strategies of device updates
solution. One is security.

134
00:05:16,465 --> 00:05:18,460
It's important to
remember or think about

135
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:20,725
the fact that device
update can be a backdoor.

136
00:05:20,725 --> 00:05:23,290
If someone's able to replace
the firmware on your device,

137
00:05:23,290 --> 00:05:25,120
then obviously there'll be
able to do whatever they

138
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:27,115
want with the device.

139
00:05:27,115 --> 00:05:29,680
You want to avoid the case where

140
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:32,260
a hacker would be
able to replace

141
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:35,290
the content of any
part of the software

142
00:05:35,290 --> 00:05:36,430
that's running on
your device with

143
00:05:36,430 --> 00:05:39,370
something that should
not be running.

144
00:05:39,370 --> 00:05:42,790
An important thing to think
about with respect to this,

145
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:46,045
and I've seen this mistake
made during my career,

146
00:05:46,045 --> 00:05:49,465
that obfuscation is
different than cryptography.

147
00:05:49,465 --> 00:05:51,700
In other words, don't

148
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:54,430
assume that by making
something difficult to do,

149
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:55,720
that means that you're
going to be the

150
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,595
only one who's going
to be able to do it.

151
00:05:57,595 --> 00:05:59,635
If people are motivated enough,

152
00:05:59,635 --> 00:06:02,650
and especially financially
motivated enough,

153
00:06:02,650 --> 00:06:04,570
there are a lot of
really clever people out

154
00:06:04,570 --> 00:06:06,640
there that can
figure out a lot of

155
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:08,560
things that might
seem like they're

156
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:11,275
really difficult to figure
out from your perspective.

157
00:06:11,275 --> 00:06:12,970
When we talk about security,

158
00:06:12,970 --> 00:06:16,240
we always want to talk about
cryptographic security.

159
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:19,060
We want to talk about
the security that people

160
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:22,480
spend a lifetime doing
PhD dissertations on,

161
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:24,719
that are able to mathematically

162
00:06:24,719 --> 00:06:28,325
prove these are extremely
complicated to break.

163
00:06:28,325 --> 00:06:32,740
It's not a matter of knowing
some secret information.

164
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:35,410
It's just that the design
of the system is robust

165
00:06:35,410 --> 00:06:39,110
enough to stand up to
security based attack.

166
00:06:39,270 --> 00:06:41,710
Second thing that you
want to think about with

167
00:06:41,710 --> 00:06:44,785
the device updates
solution is reliability.

168
00:06:44,785 --> 00:06:46,120
Especially when
we're talking about

169
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:49,000
OTA updates and the fact

170
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,040
that the payload might be

171
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:53,545
delivered over a
wireless connection,

172
00:06:53,545 --> 00:06:55,525
while a wireless
connection can fail.

173
00:06:55,525 --> 00:06:57,955
Wired connection can fail
from that perspective too,

174
00:06:57,955 --> 00:07:01,135
but a wireless could probably
even more likely fail.

175
00:07:01,135 --> 00:07:03,400
You need to have some
level of resiliency

176
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:05,455
built into your device
updates so that you could,

177
00:07:05,455 --> 00:07:07,000
for instance, start an update.

178
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,470
Then maybe I'll lose power

179
00:07:08,470 --> 00:07:10,090
or maybe I'll lose connectivity.

180
00:07:10,090 --> 00:07:12,250
I needed a way to be
able to recover from

181
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:14,650
that scenario and ensure that I

182
00:07:14,650 --> 00:07:17,380
don't end up in the
infamous brick scenario

183
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:18,550
where my device is no longer

184
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:20,425
usable. I have to ship it back.

185
00:07:20,425 --> 00:07:23,155
You can find lots of examples of

186
00:07:23,155 --> 00:07:25,000
software device
updates that went

187
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,935
wrong in this perspective,

188
00:07:26,935 --> 00:07:29,530
and big recalls that
had to happen of

189
00:07:29,530 --> 00:07:31,120
devices that were turned

190
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:32,860
into bricks through
device update.

191
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:35,605
This is something you
definitely need to think about.

192
00:07:35,605 --> 00:07:39,175
Be careful about
and work to avoid.

193
00:07:39,175 --> 00:07:41,860
Then you need to think
about the ease of use for

194
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:44,545
both end users as
well as developers.

195
00:07:44,545 --> 00:07:46,360
Think about from a
developer perspective

196
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:47,770
how many times you're
going to be updating

197
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:51,460
device during the development
process, probably a lot.

198
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:53,320
Probably multiple times per day.

199
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:55,915
If there's ways you
can streamline that,

200
00:07:55,915 --> 00:07:59,365
and make it take 30 seconds
instead of five minutes,

201
00:07:59,365 --> 00:08:03,080
that really starts to pay
off over the long-term.

202
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:06,625
From that same perspective
you can think about,

203
00:08:06,625 --> 00:08:08,215
if you're update process

204
00:08:08,215 --> 00:08:10,330
from a developer or
a user perspective,

205
00:08:10,330 --> 00:08:12,340
means I need to dig
out a serial cable

206
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:14,710
and I need to get a
USB to serial adapter.

207
00:08:14,710 --> 00:08:15,970
You should have alarm bells

208
00:08:15,970 --> 00:08:17,470
going off saying
this is probably

209
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:20,620
not a good sustainable
long-term solution.

210
00:08:20,620 --> 00:08:22,375
Might be a good way to demo out

211
00:08:22,375 --> 00:08:25,105
some initial support on
a particular platform,

212
00:08:25,105 --> 00:08:27,040
but even from a
development perspective,

213
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:28,630
you're going to want to
be replacing that with

214
00:08:28,630 --> 00:08:30,760
something a little
bit more robust

215
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:35,990
and also more efficient
in a pretty short while.

216
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:40,675
One thing that I've
noticed in terms of

217
00:08:40,675 --> 00:08:42,520
designing a device
updates solution is

218
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:44,995
that really a device
updates solution,

219
00:08:44,995 --> 00:08:47,215
the best ones are the
ones that are invisible,

220
00:08:47,215 --> 00:08:49,840
but nobody really sees and
nobody really knows about,

221
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,490
because they always
just work and

222
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:53,200
they do exactly what
they're supposed to do.

223
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:54,610
From a user perspective,

224
00:08:54,610 --> 00:08:57,175
it's transparent and not

225
00:08:57,175 --> 00:08:58,540
really noticeable
the fact that you're

226
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:00,490
doing updates to begin with.

227
00:09:00,490 --> 00:09:02,980
Really the way to grade how good

228
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:05,665
your device update scheme is,

229
00:09:05,665 --> 00:09:07,900
all the things that
it doesn't do.

230
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:10,300
It doesn't expose your end users

231
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:11,949
or your company to hackers.

232
00:09:11,949 --> 00:09:14,110
It doesn't turn your
devices into bricks

233
00:09:14,110 --> 00:09:16,315
when bad things
happen during update,

234
00:09:16,315 --> 00:09:18,010
doesn't burden your customers or

235
00:09:18,010 --> 00:09:20,920
your developers with extra
steps that they have to do,

236
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:26,875
or over burden type of
procedure for update devices.

237
00:09:26,875 --> 00:09:30,010
To use the analogy of
American football,

238
00:09:30,010 --> 00:09:33,730
it's really the equivalent
of the long snapper.

239
00:09:33,730 --> 00:09:36,760
A long snapper in
American football is like

240
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:39,880
a really high percentage
success case.

241
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:42,040
Where the snapper snaps

242
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:44,470
the ball to the punter
and the punter kicks

243
00:09:44,470 --> 00:09:46,480
the ball to the opposing team

244
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:49,705
and 99 plus percent of the time,

245
00:09:49,705 --> 00:09:51,230
you don't know anything about

246
00:09:51,230 --> 00:09:53,850
the long snapper as the player.

247
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:56,370
It's just assumed that
they're going to do their job

248
00:09:56,370 --> 00:09:59,385
perfectly and there won't
be any issue with the snap.

249
00:09:59,385 --> 00:10:01,965
But when there is an
issue with the snap,

250
00:10:01,965 --> 00:10:03,395
with the long snap,

251
00:10:03,395 --> 00:10:06,310
it's a really bad
situation usually and

252
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:07,690
this is analogous to

253
00:10:07,690 --> 00:10:09,595
what can happen with
the device update.

254
00:10:09,595 --> 00:10:11,470
You want something that's robust

255
00:10:11,470 --> 00:10:13,060
and reliable that you don't ever

256
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:16,265
know about and if you mess
up and you do know about it,

257
00:10:16,265 --> 00:10:19,790
then it can really
cause a big problem.

258
00:10:19,790 --> 00:10:21,530
We'll talk more in
the next videos

259
00:10:21,530 --> 00:10:23,940
about how to avoid
this scenario.